  "strategy": "Approach and rationale:\n\nOur strategy aims to improve upon the previous program's score of 1.506325 by using a frequency-domain optimization approach combined with efficient matrix operations to minimize the maximum concentration. The core idea is to leverage signal processing techniques to find sequences that minimize the autocorrelation peak while respecting the coefficient constraints. This differs from the previous neural-network-based template suggestions by being fundamentally different in algorithm choice.\n\nWe will:\n1. Treat the problem as minimizing the maximum concentration in the autocorrelation\n2. Use a lookup table of optimal configurations from the frequency domain\n3. Transform the problem into an optimized parameter space\n4. Use gradient-based optimization in the frequency-domain space\n5. Make intelligent choices about the sequence structure based on signal properties\n\nThe main advantage is that this approach is significantly more efficient and can handle larger sequences without the same level of exploration required by traditional coordinate descent methods.\n\nMathematical specification:\n\nThe problem requires us to minimize:\nscore = 2n * max(autocorrelation(a,a)) / (sum(a))\u00b2\n\nOur transformation will define a sequence in the frequency domain as a trigonometric polynomial with parameters (A, B, C) such that:\na(t) = sum_{i=1}^3 [A_i * cos(\u03c9_i * t + \u03c6_i) + B_i * sin(\u03c9_i * t + \u03b8_i)]\n\nWhere t runs from 1 to n.\n\nConstraints include:\n1. Non-negative coefficients\n2. Minimum sum of 0.01\n3. Individual coefficients between 0 and 1000\n\nThe optimization will be over the discrete frequency components, making it a linear problem in some parameter space.\n\nImplementation procedure:\n\nWe'll implement a multi-stage optimization:\n\n1. Aux: FrequencyLookup - Precompute a database of autocorrelation peaks versus sequence properties using FFT convolution\n2. CandidateGeneration: TrigonometricPolynomial - Generate candidate sequences as linear combinations of sine/cosine waves\n3. InitialBest - Find the best initial configuration using brute-force over small sets of frequencies\n4. Phase1GradientDescent - Use gradient information from the autocorrelation peaks to optimize the parameters\n5. Phase2IterativeRefinement - Refine candidate solutions using golden-section search for the minimum convex combination\n6. Phase3StructureOptimization - Adjust sequence structure based on periodicity and correlation properties\n7. Phase4LengthAdaptation - Dynamically adjust sequence length to 500-1200 based on best score\n\nWe'll structure the code with helper functions that project sequences to the valid feature space, compute transitions using convolution, and transform between coefficient and frequency spaces. The main `propose_candidate` function will coordinate these stages and ensure we stay within the 1500-second time budget.\n\nThe algorithm will be efficient because:\n\u2022 We're operating in a compact frequency-domain parameterization\n\u2022 Convolution is accelerated using FFT\n\u2022 We maintain a validated best sequence to ensure progress is real\n\nLet's specify each stage with concrete steps.",
